That additional constraint makes the design physically impossible with certainty.

A sealed, thermally isolated room has no permitted path for the rack’s continuous 10 kW output to leave. The energy balance is:

\[
E(t)=E(0)+10{,}000t\ \text{J}
\]

The room and rack must therefore heat continuously. Any internal device can only redistribute or temporarily store the heat; it cannot maintain ambient temperature indefinitely without an external sink, exported mass, radiative escape path, or energy-consuming refrigeration.

No valid portfolio exists within the stated inventory and constraints. At least one must change:

- allow heat transfer through the room boundary;
- allow radiation out of the room;
- allow ventilation or fluid export;
- allow a heat sink or phase-change storage for a finite duration;
- permit the rack or room to rise above ambient;
- reduce continuous power below 10 kW.
